 {
  "id": "add",
  "grade": 5,
  "unit": "Fractions",
  "title": "Adding fractions",
  "pre": "g4-likefrac",
  "story": true,
  "final": [
   -1
  ],
  "learn": [
   {
    "h": "Why can't we just add the bottoms?",
    "text": "(1)/(2) + (1)/(3) One halfOne third The bottom number tells you the size of the pieces. Halves and thirds are different sizes, so we can't add them yet.And 2/5 is wrong: it's less than one half!"
   },
   {
    "h": "Cut them into same size pieces",
    "text": "1/2 = 3/61/3 = 2/6 Find the smallest number both bottoms go into. 2 and 3 both go into 6. That's the least common denominator. To turn halves into sixths we multiply the bottom by 3, so we multiply the top by 3 too."
   },
   {
    "h": "Now add the tops",
    "text": "(3)/(6) + (2)/(6) = (5)/(6) Same size pieces: 3 sixths + 2 sixths = 5 sixths. The bottom stays 6. Every problem goes in 5 steps: find the LCD, rewrite the first, rewrite the second, add, simplify. Your finished steps stay on the page like written work."
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "id": "sub",
  "grade": 5,
  "unit": "Fractions",
  "title": "Subtracting fractions",
  "pre": "g4-likefrac",
  "story": true,
  "final": [
   -1
  ],
  "learn": [
   {
    "h": "Subtracting works the same way",
    "text": "(1)/(2) − (1)/(3) 1/2 = 3/61/3 = 2/6 Before we can take away, the pieces have to be the same size. Find the least common denominator, 6, and rewrite both fractions."
   },
   {
    "h": "Take away the tops",
    "text": "(3)/(6) − (2)/(6) = (1)/(6) 3 sixths take away 2 sixths leaves 1 sixth. The bottom stays 6. Never subtract the bottoms: 6 − 6 would make 0, and you can't cut something into 0 pieces."
   }
  ]
 },
